\value{
The function returns an object of class \code{"phylo"}: the backbone phylogeny with the grafted taxa added as new tips.
Backbone branch lengths are untouched, and the grafted phylogeny is ultrametric.

In addition to the standard elements of a \code{"phylo"} object, the phylogeny has the following elements:
\itemize{
\item \code{grafted_tips} Named logical vector with one value per tip, in the order of \verb{$tip.label}. \code{TRUE} for grafted tips. Names are the tip labels.
\item \code{grafted_edges} Named logical vector with one value per branch, in the order of the rows of \verb{$edge}. \code{TRUE} for grafted branches,
i.e., branches with only grafted taxa below them. Names are the IDs of the tipward nodes of the branches (\verb{$edge[, 2]}).
Use them to color the phylogeny on plots (see examples).
}

\strong{These elements are not updated by functions that modify the phylogeny} (e.g., \code{ape::drop.tip()}, which removes tips but keeps the other elements as they are,
and \code{ape::write.tree()}, which removes them). Use \code{\link[=identify_grafted]{phylograftR::identify_grafted()}} to recompute \code{grafted_tips} and \code{grafted_edges} on a manipulated phylogeny.

Optional elements can be added to the output based on what optional argument was provided:
\itemize{
\item \code{"BD_model_summary"} (attribute). Only if \code{return_BD_model_summary = TRUE}. Data.frame with one row per birth-death model fitted (one per target clade),
summarizing information about the clade used for model fitting, diversification rates, and sampling fraction.
It can be extracted a posteriori with \code{\link[=extract_BD_model_summary]{phylograftR::extract_BD_model_summary()}}.
\item \code{simmap} Only if a \code{simmap} is provided and \code{return_simmap = TRUE}. The updated stochastic character map of the grafted phylogeny (class \code{"simmap"}):
the pieces of the branches of the backbone keep their states (cut where taxa were grafted), and the grafted branch is entirely in the state of the point where it was grafted.
With \code{treat_states_as_ranges = TRUE}, the grafted branch is entirely in the range it inherited (see 'Biogeographic ranges as states' in Details section).
This element can be plotted as any \code{simmap}, e.g., \code{plot(grafted_phy$simmap)}, or used to extract the states of the tips with \code{phytools::getStates(grafted_phy$simmap, type = "tips")}.
It has the same exact topology and branch lengths as the grafted phylogeny.
With the maps of several traits, \verb{$simmap} is a list of such simmaps, one per trait, named after the traits (see 'Several traits' in Details section).
}

With \code{nb_simulations > 1}, the function returns an object of class \code{"multiPhylo"} with one grafted phylogeny per simulation, named \code{"sim_1"}, \code{"sim_2"}, ...
Each phylogeny has the elements described above (including \verb{$simmap} if a \code{simmap} was provided and \code{return_simmap = TRUE}).
The \code{"multiPhylo"} object has the attributes \code{"seeds"} (named vector of the seeds of the phylogenies, to reproduce each of them alone with \code{nb_simulations = 1};
\code{NULL} if \code{seed = NULL}) and \code{"failed"} (data.frame with the seed and the reason of the simulations that failed, 0 row if none).

With multiple stochastic maps (i.e., a \code{"multiSimmap"}) provided as \code{simmap}, the function returns a list with one element per map (named after the maps):
a phylogeny if \code{nb_simulations = 1}, a \code{"multiPhylo"} otherwise (see 'Multiple stochastic maps').
The list has the attribute \code{"failed_maps"} (data.frame with the \code{map} and the \code{reason} of the maps without any simulation, dropped from the output).
The phylogenies have the attributes \code{"seed"} (their seed, unless \code{seed = NULL}) and, with several maps, \code{"map"}, to ensure reproducibility of each simulation.
}
\description{
This function grafts missing taxa onto an ultrametric, time-calibrated backbone phylogeny.
The position of each taxon is constrained by its taxonomic assignment (the \strong{target clade}, defined in \code{Target_clade}),
and optionally by taxonomic exclusions (the \strong{excluded subclades}, defined in \code{Excluded_subclades}),
and/or exclusions based on species attributes (the \strong{allowed states}, defined in \code{allowed_states} based on the \code{simmap}).

The divergence times of the grafted branches are either drawn uniformly along the branches (\code{method = "uniform"}),
or drawn from a birth-death model fitted on the target clade (\code{method = "BD_clades"}, default).
\strong{The backbone is never modified: only new tips are added.}
}
\details{
\subsection{Stop conditions}{

The function \strong{stops, before any grafting,} if the backbone is not ultrametric, or is not binary (i.e., all nodes have only two descendant lineages).
It also stops if some taxa have no branch available for grafting. The taxa concerned are listed in the error message,
so the constraints (\code{include_target_clade_stem}, \code{Excluded_subclades}, \code{exclude_subclades_stem} or, with a \code{simmap}, the allowed states) can be adjusted.
With \code{method = "BD_clades"}, the function can also stop during grafting if no usable divergence age is found after \code{max_age_redraws} redraws (see 'Methods').
}

\subsection{Target clades, stem regions and excluded subclades}{

Each taxon is grafted within its target clade. The branches available for grafting are all the branches of the target clade
(descending from its MRCA node), plus its stem region if the stem is available for this taxon (\code{missing_taxa_list$include_target_clade_stem} of the taxon if provided, otherwise the global argument \code{include_target_clade_stem}).
\strong{Grafting on the stem branch places the taxon sister to the target clade.}
Taxa of the same target clade can therefore differ in their stem availability: the clade is the same (same MRCA node, same richness information),
but the branches and the time window available for the divergence times of these taxa differ.

\strong{The stem region is not restricted to the original stem branch.} A taxon grafted on the stem splits it in two pieces, and becomes itself part of the stem region:
later taxa can be grafted on both pieces of the stem, and on the terminal branch of the earlier taxon (the two taxa are then sister, and together sister to the target clade).
More generally, the stem region includes all the branches of the 'missing-only subtrees' hanging from the stem, i.e., subtrees containing only taxa of \code{missing_taxa_list$Taxon_name},
whatever their own target clade. This mirrors what happens within the crown group, where missing taxa can already be grafted on the branches of other missing taxa.

\strong{Branches inside excluded subclades are removed from the pool of available branches.} By default (\code{exclude_subclades_stem = TRUE}),
the stem region of the excluded subclades is removed as well: the taxon can be neither nested within the excluded subclade,
nor sister to it (nor sister to the missing taxa already grafted on its stem).
This is the only way to exclude a single tip, as its terminal branch is its only branch.
With \code{exclude_subclades_stem = FALSE} (globally, or for a given subclade in \code{missing_taxa_list$exclude_subclades_stem}), the stem of the excluded subclade stays available:
the taxon can be sister to the excluded subclade, but never nested within it. A single tip then excludes nothing.
The setting is specific to each excluded subclade, so a taxon can have excluded subclades with different settings.

\strong{Excluded subclades are expected to be nested within their target clade}. The function checks their position before grafting:
a warning is issued if an excluded subclade is outside the target clade (the exclusion then has no effect).
The function stops if an excluded subclade contains the whole target clade, or is identical to it: no branch (or only the stem branch) would be left for grafting.
To graft taxa specifically on the stem branch of a clade or of a single tip, use \code{\link[=graft_missing_clades]{phylograftR::graft_missing_clades()}} instead.
A warning lists the single-tip excluded subclades with a stem that is not excluded, as they exclude nothing.
}

\subsection{Methods}{
\itemize{
\item \strong{\code{"uniform"}}: Taxa are grafted one by one, per target clade from the smallest to the largest, so nested clades are processed first (see below).
Within each target clade, taxa are grafted in random order. The branch used for grafting is drawn among the available branches
with probabilities proportional to branch lengths, and the position on the branch is drawn from a uniform distribution.
This is fast, but does not account for diversification dynamics.
Note that under a birth-death process, the probability of a side lineage splitting off a branch at age t follows the exponential function e^(-rt),
with r the net diversification rate (speciation minus extinction).
A uniform draw implicitly assumes a flat density (r = 0), and gives too much weight to old divergence times compared to the expectation from a birth-death process.
\item \strong{\code{"BD_clades"}}: Taxa are grafted per target clade, from the smallest to the largest, so nested clades are processed first (see below).
For each target clade, a birth-death model is fitted with \code{phytools::fit.bd()} on the clade, or on the smallest enclosing clade with at least \code{min_tips} tips.
The sampling fraction accounts for the number of missing taxa provided for grafting in this clade.
The divergence times of all taxa missing in the target clade are then jointly drawn with \code{TreeSim::corsim()}, between the present and the stem age of the clade
(or its crown age if the stem is not available for these taxa).
Taxa are grafted from the oldest to the youngest divergence time following this procedure:
\enumerate{
\item A taxon is grafted at its age if an available branch in an allowed state crosses this age in the current tree.
\item The ages for which no such valid branch piece exists are discarded.
\item The ages of the taxa left are drawn again jointly, and grafted or discarded in the same way.
This is repeated until all the taxa of the group are grafted, up to \code{max_age_redraws} times.
Taxa of the same target clade with different stem availability (or, with a \code{simmap}, different allowed states) are drawn in separate groups,
and the birth-death model is fitted only once per target clade.
\strong{\code{"BD_clades"} is the recommended grafting method}, but it can be time consuming for large phylogenies.
}
}

With both methods, \strong{nested clades are processed first}, so the taxa of a larger clade are grafted on a tree that already
includes the taxa of the clades nested within it, and can be grafted on their branches.
For taxa of the same clade, those without access to the stem come first.
For \code{"BD_clades"}, it also ensures that the divergence times of nested clades are accounted for when drawing the divergence times of the larger clade.
Taxa of the same target clade are grafted in random order, so they are exchangeable.
}

\subsection{Enclosing clades}{

Under the \code{"BD_clades"} method, when a target clade contains fewer tips than \code{min_tips}, an alternative higher clade is selected to fit the birth-death model.
This 'enclosing clade' is identified by walking rootward the tree node by node, starting from the target clade, until a clade meets the \code{min_tips} condition.
This ensures that the diversification dynamics modeled are based on enough tips to be relevant, while being closely associated with the target clade.
}

\subsection{Sampling fraction and unsampled species}{

Under \code{method = "BD_clades"}, the birth-death model of a clade is fitted with the sampling fraction
\code{n_present / (n_present + n_pending + n_unsampled)}, with \code{n_present} the tips of the fitted clade in the current tree (backbone tips and taxa already grafted),
\code{n_pending} the taxa of \code{missing_taxa_list} still to graft in this clade, and \code{n_unsampled} the unsampled species derived from \code{total_richness_per_clades}.
Taxa grafted in nested clades processed earlier are already in the tree, so they are not counted as missing anymore.
\strong{Unsampled species are only used to fit the rates: they are not grafted}, and do not compete with the grafted taxa for the branches.

For a clade with a \code{total_richness} value provided: \code{nb_unsampled = total_richness - nb_backbone - nb_missing}, with \code{nb_missing} the number of taxa to graft
whose target clade is this clade or is nested within it. For a clade with no \code{total_richness} value (\code{NA}, or enclosing a target clade without being listed),
\code{nb_unsampled} is the sum over the largest clades nested within it with a \code{total_richness} value provided.
As such, the unsampled species of nested clades are summed across disjoint clades but never counted twice within nested clades.

If \code{total_richness_per_clades = NULL}, \code{nb_unsampled = 0}: the clades have no unsampled species (the same as providing only \code{NA} in \verb{$total_richness}).

The species actually present in each listed clade, and the resulting sampling fractions for downstream analyses, may differ among grafted phylogenies,
as a taxon can be grafted on the stem of its target clade, or within a clade nested in it, which changes the richness of these clades.
Use \code{\link[=extract_sampling_info]{phylograftR::extract_sampling_info()}} to extract them a posteriori.

The function stops, before any grafting, if \code{total_richness_per_clades} does not cover all the target clades, if a \code{total_richness} provided is lower than
the number of backbone species plus species to graft of the clade, or if a clade has fewer unsampled species than the sum of the clades with
a \code{total_richness} provided nested within it.
With \code{method = "uniform"}, no diversification model is used: \code{total_richness_per_clades} is not used during the grafting (a warning is issued).
It is only checked, and can be provided later to \code{\link[=extract_sampling_info]{phylograftR::extract_sampling_info()}}.
}

\subsection{Species attributes: allowed states of a simmap}{

A \code{simmap} describes the states of a discrete attribute (e.g., a trait or a range) along the branches of the backbone: each branch is cut in pieces, each piece being in one state.
With a \code{simmap}, \strong{each taxon can only be grafted on the pieces of the available branches that are in its allowed states}
(\code{missing_taxa_list$allowed_states} of the taxon if provided, otherwise the global argument \code{allowed_states}).
This combines with the other constraints: the available branches are those of the target clade, minus the excluded subclades, restricted to the allowed states.
If all states are allowed (all states listed in \code{allowed_states}, or no allowed states at all), grafting is not restricted by the states,
and constraints are only based on target clades and excluded subclades.

\strong{A grafted taxon is assumed to be in the state of the point of the backbone where it was grafted, along its whole branch},
and so are the taxa grafted on its branch later. Taxa grafted later can therefore be grafted on the branches of taxa grafted earlier,
if their state is allowed.

The states are updated alongside the phylogeny, and the updated \code{simmap} is returned in \verb{$simmap} if \code{return_simmap = TRUE}.
A \code{simmap} can thus be provided with \code{allowed_states = NULL}, only to get the states of the grafted taxa and branches:
grafting is then not restricted by the states and gives the same result as without \code{simmap} (for the same \code{seed}).

With \code{method = "uniform"}, the draw is uniform over the available pieces (i.e., portion of branches in the allowed states), weighted by their length:
every time point in an allowed state has the same chance to be selected.

With \code{method = "BD_clades"}, the divergence ages of the taxa of a group (same target clade, same stem availability and same allowed states) are drawn jointly
with \code{TreeSim::corsim()}, as without a \code{simmap}: the diversification model is not modified by the states.
The same grafting procedure, described above without a \code{simmap}, is then used except that
an age can only be used if an available branch in an allowed state crosses it (without a \code{simmap}, if an available branch crosses it).
The ages are used from the oldest to the youngest, each for one taxon of the group.

\strong{Cautions for interpretation: risks of circularity.}

Constraining the grafting by a trait that will then be analyzed (e.g., the association of the trait with diversification) may bias the results towards the constraint.
The grafted taxa are placed only where the trait allows it, so they are in the allowed states by construction. This can create or inflate an association between the trait and the diversification dynamics,
affect the rates of transitions between states, or inflate the phylogenetic signal of the trait. It also reduces the uncertainty about the state of the grafted lineages since they always inherit a unique state.
It is recommended to acknowledge these potential biases when interpreting such analyses.

To \strong{limit the risk of circularity}, multiple approaches are possible:
\itemize{
\item constrain the grafting by traits that are not the object of downstream analysis;
\item compare the results with and without the constraint (or on the backbone only);
\item keep the constraint as loose as the information allows (e.g., allowed states that include neighboring states);
\item use several maps and simulations to propagate the uncertainty in ancestral state estimates.
}
}

\subsection{Biogeographic ranges as states}{

With \code{treat_states_as_ranges = TRUE}, the states of the \code{simmap} are biogeographic ranges, coded as in \code{BioGeoBEARS}
(one capital letter per area, and the letters of a multi-area range in alphabetical order, e.g., \code{"AB"}),
and the allowed states are the ranges a missing clade can have.

\strong{An allowed range matches a state of the \code{simmap} if it is contained in it}: a missing taxon allowed in \code{"A"} can be grafted on branch pieces in \code{"A"}, \code{"AB"} or \code{"ABC"},
whereas a missing taxon allowed in \code{"AB"} only can be grafted on branch pieces in \code{"AB"} or \code{"ABC"}.

When multiple allowed states are provided, the inherited range is drawn randomly from the allowed ranges enclosed within the range of the branch piece where it is grafted.
When grafting on an edge with a larger range than the inherited range (e.g., \code{"A"} on \code{"AB"}), this results in a subset sympatric speciation event such as (AB) -> (AB), (A).
In the returned \code{simmap}, the branches of the grafted lineage are in their inherited range, whereas the branch it is grafted on continues in its own range.
}

\subsection{Multiple simulations}{

With \code{nb_simulations > 1}, the grafting is simulated \code{nb_simulations} times from the same backbone and the same \code{missing_taxa_list}.
Each simulation starts again from the backbone: the birth-death models are fitted on the tree of the simulation (they depend on the taxa already grafted),
and the simulations do not share anything but the inputs. The phylogenies are returned in a \code{"multiPhylo"} object (a single \code{"phylo"} if \code{nb_simulations = 1}),
and the memory used grows with the number of simulations.

\strong{The first simulation uses \code{seed}}, so it is the same phylogeny as a run with \code{nb_simulations = 1} and the same \code{seed}.
The seeds of the other simulations are drawn from a stream started by \code{seed}, and returned in the attribute \code{"seeds"}:
a simulation can be reproduced alone with its seed, and the first simulations of a longer run are the same as the ones of a shorter run.
With \code{seed = NULL}, the simulations follow the random number generator of the session.

A failure that is specific to a simulation, because of the random draws (no usable divergence age or time after \code{max_age_redraws} redraws), skips this simulation with a warning:
the name of a phylogeny keeps the index of its simulation (e.g., \code{"sim_3"} is missing if the third simulation failed). The function reports the number of phylogenies obtained
if it differs from \code{nb_simulations}, and stops if no simulation succeeded. With \code{force_simulations = TRUE}, new seeds are tried until \code{nb_simulations} phylogenies are obtained
(up to \code{10 * nb_simulations} attempts): the phylogenies are then named \code{"sim_1"} to \code{"sim_N"} without gaps, and the seeds of the failed attempts are in the attribute \code{"failed"}.
Any other error (invalid inputs, no branch available for a taxon, ...) concerns all the simulations and stops the whole run.

With \code{save_temp = TRUE}, each simulation saves its own temporary file (see 'Temporary saves and resuming').
}

\subsection{Multiple stochastic maps}{

When multiple stochastic maps of the same trait are provided as a \code{"multiSimmap"}, the grafting is run with each of its maps.
The maps are alternative hypotheses of the evolution of the same trait (or range), used as alternative constraints.
\strong{All the maps and the simulations are crossed}: each map is used for \code{nb_simulations} simulations, so \code{P} maps give \code{P * nb_simulations} grafted phylogenies
This can lead to a quick inflation of computation time, thus a warning is sent if the total number of grafted phylogenies (\code{P * nb_simulations}) is more than \strong{1000}.

The output is a list with one element per stochastic map, named after the maps (the names of the \code{"multiSimmap"} if it has some, otherwise \code{"map_1"}, ..., \code{"map_P"}).
With \code{nb_simulations = 1}, each element is a grafted phylogeny. With several simulations, each element is a \code{"multiPhylo"} (see 'Multiple simulations')
with phylogenies named \code{"<map>_sim_1"}, \code{"<map>_sim_2"}, ... and the attributes \code{"seeds"} and \code{"failed"} of the map.
Each phylogeny can be reproduced alone with its map (as a single \code{simmap}) and its seed.

A map for which no simulation could be obtained is dropped from the output with a warning, and listed in the attribute \code{"failed_maps"} of the list
(data.frame with the columns \code{map} and \code{reason}). The function stops if no map gives any phylogeny.

With \code{save_temp = TRUE}, each map and each simulation saves its own temporary file (see 'Temporary saves and resuming').
}

\subsection{Several traits}{

The maps of several traits can be used at once to constrain grafting. \code{simmap} is then a list with one element per trait, each a \code{"simmap"} or a \code{"multiSimmap"}
(e.g., \code{simmap = list(color = color_simmap, elevation = elevation_simmap)}). The elements can be named after the traits (all or none, with different names that cannot contain \verb{;}, \verb{,}, \code{=} or \code{|}),
otherwise the traits are named \code{"trait_1"}, \code{"trait_2"}, ... All the maps must have the same tips, topology and branch lengths as the backbone \code{phy}.

\strong{A taxon can only be grafted on the parts of the branches that are in one of its allowed states, for every trait.}
The allowed states are provided for each trait, in the order of the list of maps (or named after the traits):
the global argument \code{allowed_states} is a list with one element per trait, e.g., \code{list(c("blue", "green"), NULL, "high")} for three traits,
and \code{missing_taxa_list$allowed_states} has such a list for each taxon.
A trait with \code{NULL} (or an empty vector, or \code{NA}) has no taxon-level value, so the global \code{allowed_states} of the trait is used for the taxon
(a taxon-level value replaces the global one, trait by trait, as with a single trait).
A trait with no states, neither at the taxon level nor globally, has all its states allowed (no restriction).
Other traits can still be restricted: \code{list(NULL, "high")} only restricts the second trait.

With \code{return_simmap = TRUE}, \verb{$simmap} is a list of updated simmaps, one per trait, named after the traits.
In each simmap, a grafted branch is entirely in the state of each trait at the point where it was grafted (each trait is inherited from the lineage it was grafted on).

With a list of \code{"multiSimmap"} provided as \code{simmap}, the maps of the traits are paired: the grafting is run with the map 1 of every trait, then the map 2 of every trait, and so on.
\strong{All the traits must therefore have the same number of maps} to be associated in a given grafting process.

One of the traits can be treated as biogeographic ranges combined with any number of other traits.
Use a logical vector in \code{treat_states_as_ranges} to define which of the trait is to be treated as biogeographic ranges for inheritance.
The range logic is the one of 'Biogeographic ranges as states' for the range trait:
its allowed values for grafting are any ranges that encompass the allowed range.
For instance, a missing taxon allowed in \code{"A;high"} can be grafted on branch pieces in \code{"A;high"}, \code{"AB;high"} or \code{"ABC;high"},
whereas a missing taxon allowed in \code{"AB;high"} only can be grafted on branch pieces in \code{"AB;high"} or \code{"ABC;high"}.

The other traits are matched exactly, and are inherited as they are from the point where the taxon is grafted: with \code{"AB"} and \code{"high"} at the graft point and the allowed range \code{"A"},
the taxon inherits \code{"A"} and \code{"high"}. \strong{Only one trait can be treated as ranges} to prevent inflation of the number of combined allowed states to consider.
}

\subsection{Parallel computation}{

The simulations based on the different stochastic maps and seeds are independent, so they can be run in parallel with the package \code{future} and \code{future.apply}.
\strong{Both packages must be installed independently from phylograftR}.

The grafting function does not manage the workers: \strong{you must set a plan beforehand}, e.g., \code{future::plan(future::multisession, workers = 4)},
and reset it afterwards with \code{future::plan(future::sequential)}. With the default \code{parallel = NULL}, the function uses the plan if there is one, \code{parallel = FALSE} ignores it.
All the \code{P * nb_simulations} simulations (with \code{P} stochastic maps per trait) are run as one set of tasks, so the workers are used evenly.

The output is exactly the same as without parallelization.
The messages, warnings and printed output of the simulations are relayed to the session when the tasks are collected (so they appear after the simulations, not live),
and with \code{verbose = TRUE} one line is printed when each simulation is done.

Setting up the workers and sending the data to them has a time cost, so \strong{parallelization only pays off with large backbone phylogenies, or many simulations or maps}.
As a rule of thumb, use parallel computation when the sequential run would last more than a minute or so, i.e., for large backbone phylogenies (500 tips or more) and several grafted phylogenies
(\code{nb_maps * nb_simulations}), or for 50 or more grafted phylogenies on small backbones.
}

\subsection{Temporary saves and resuming}{

With \code{save_temp = TRUE}, each grafted phylogeny (one per simulation and per stochastic map) is saved in its own \code{.rds} file, built from \code{temp_file_path} by adding
the suffixes \code{"_map_<name>"} when a \code{"multiSimmap"} is used, then \code{"_sim_<k>"} when \code{nb_simulations > 1}.
For instance, with \code{temp_file_path = "dir/temp.rds"}:
\itemize{
\item a single phylogeny uses \code{"dir/temp.rds"};
\item several simulations use \code{"dir/temp_sim_1.rds"}, \code{"dir/temp_sim_2.rds"}, ...;
\item several maps use \code{"dir/temp_map_A.rds"}, \code{"dir/temp_map_B.rds"}, ...;
\item several simulations across several maps use both suffixes as \code{"dir/temp_map_A_sim_1.rds"}.
}

With \code{force_simulations = TRUE}, \verb{<k>} is the number of the attempt, not necessarily the ID to which the grafted phylogeny will be attributed to in the final output.
This is required to identify the appropriate seed used to produce each attempt; attempts that failed leave a file too for tractability.

Temporary files can be read with \code{readRDS()} and used to plot the grafted phylogeny in its current state.
The object is a regular \code{"phylo"} with the attribute \code{"phylograftR_temp"} that stores what is needed to resume a run.
Once the phylogeny is fully grafted, the file is replaced by the complete output of the simulation (with \verb{$simmap}, \verb{$grafted_tips}, etc.).

With \code{clean_temp_files = TRUE} (default), all the temporary files of the run are deleted once the function returns without crashing.
\strong{A run that crashes or is interrupted never deletes its files}, so it can be resumed with \code{resume = TRUE}.
Use \code{clean_temp_files = FALSE} to keep the files after a successful run.

With \code{resume = TRUE}, the function must be called again with \strong{exactly the same arguments} as the interrupted run.
For each phylogeny, its temporary file is searched: a complete phylogeny is reused as it is, an unfinished one is continued from its last save, and a phylogeny without file is grafted from scratch.

\strong{Resuming is not equivalent to grafting the taxa left on the temporary phylogeny used as a new backbone}, with a \code{missing_taxa_list} restricted to these taxa:
the taxa already grafted would be treated as backbone species, so the sampling fractions of the birth-death models, the stem of the target clades,
the identification of the grafted tips and the updated \code{simmap} would be wrong, and a \code{simmap} of the initial backbone would not match the temporary phylogeny.
Use \code{resume = TRUE} instead.
}
}
\examples{
# --- Prepare data --- #

# Create a toy backbone phylogeny: ultrametric, with a root age of 50 time units
set.seed(1234)
phy <- ape::rcoal(n = 30)
phy$edge.length <- phy$edge.length / max(ape::node.depth.edgelength(phy)) * 50

# List the missing taxa to graft
missing_taxa_list <- list(
  Taxon_name = c("X1", "X2", "X3", "X4"),
  # Target clades defined by pairs of tips (MRCA), or by a single tip
  # All the other items are named after Taxon_name (same names, same order)
  Target_clade = list(X1 = c("t26", "t18"), X2 = c("t8", "t22"), X3 = "t5", X4 = c("t26", "t18")),
  # Stem of the target clade available for grafting?
  # NA uses the global 'include_target_clade_stem' argument (TRUE by default)
  # X4 cannot be grafted on the stem
  include_target_clade_stem = c(X1 = NA, X2 = NA, X3 = NA, X4 = FALSE),
  # Excluded subclades defined by pairs of tips. Only X1 has an excluded subclade
  # (the optional name of the excluded subclade, here 'clade_A', is used in messages)
  Excluded_subclades = list(X1 = list(clade_A = c("t10", "t19")), X2 = NULL, X3 = NULL, X4 = NULL),
  # Stem of the excluded subclades also excluded?
  # NA / NULL use the global 'exclude_subclades_stem' argument (TRUE by default).
  # Here the stem of the excluded subclade of X1 stays available,
  # so X1 can be sister to it, but never nested within it.
  exclude_subclades_stem = list(X1 = FALSE, X2 = NULL, X3 = NULL, X4 = NULL))

# --- Perform grafting --- #

# Graft with the default method ("BD_clades")
grafted_phy <- graft_missing_taxa(phy = phy, missing_taxa_list = missing_taxa_list,
                                  method = "BD_clades", verbose = FALSE, seed = 1234)
plot(grafted_phy, cex = 0.7)

# Highlight the grafted tips and branches in red
plot_grafted(grafted_phy, cex = 0.7)

# Graft with the "uniform" method, without using the excluded subclades
grafted_phy_uniform <- graft_missing_taxa(phy = phy, missing_taxa_list = missing_taxa_list,
                                          use_excluded_subclades = FALSE,
                                          method = "uniform", verbose = FALSE, seed = 1234)

# Check that the constraints are respected
check_grafting_constraints(phy_grafted = grafted_phy, phy_backbone = phy,
                           missing_taxa_list = missing_taxa_list)


# --- Produce several grafted phylogenies --- #

# The grafting is stochastic: several simulations can be run at once from the same backbone and list
# (a "multiPhylo" named sim_1, sim_2, ...; sim_1 is the same as a single run with the same seed)
grafted_phys <- graft_missing_taxa(phy = phy, missing_taxa_list = missing_taxa_list,
                                   method = "uniform", nb_simulations = 3,
                                   verbose = FALSE, seed = 1234)
names(grafted_phys)
# Seeds of the simulations, to reproduce each phylogeny alone
attr(grafted_phys, "seeds")

# Plot phylos
old_par <- par()["mfrow"]
par(mfrow = c(1, 3))
plot_grafted(grafted_phys, index = 1, cex = 0.7, main = "Sim #1") # sim_1
plot_grafted(grafted_phys, index = 2, cex = 0.7, main = "Sim #2") # sim_2
plot_grafted(grafted_phys, index = 3, cex = 0.7, main = "Sim #3") # sim_3
par(old_par)


# --- Account for unsampled species --- #

# Account for unsampled species with the total richness of the clades.
# All the unique target clades must be listed
# (NA = unknown richness, i.e., no unsampled species beyond nested clades)
total_richness_per_clades <- list(
  Target_clade = list(c("t26", "t18"), c("t8", "t22"), "t5"),
  total_richness = c(25, NA, 3))
grafted_phy_richness <- graft_missing_taxa(phy = phy, missing_taxa_list = missing_taxa_list,
                                           total_richness_per_clades = total_richness_per_clades,
                                           method = "BD_clades", verbose = FALSE, seed = 1234)

# Inspect the resulting clade richness and sampling fractions
extract_sampling_info(phy_grafted = grafted_phy_richness,
                      total_richness_per_clades = total_richness_per_clades)


# --- Apply attribute-based exclusions --- #

# Restrict the grafting to some states of a stochastic character map (simmap) of the backbone
# The toy simmap has three states, "blue", "green" and "red"
data(toy_backbone)
data(toy_simmap)
data(toy_missing_taxa_list_with_allowed_states)

# This missing_taxa_list has the item 'allowed_states',
# with the states where each taxon can be grafted
# (NULL for the taxa without a specific value).
toy_missing_taxa_list_with_allowed_states$allowed_states

# Perform grafting informed with the simmap and associated 'allowed_states'.
# The taxa without taxon-level states use the global 'allowed_states' argument:
# here they can only be grafted on "blue" or "green" sections of the branches
grafted_phy_states <- graft_missing_taxa(phy = toy_backbone,
   missing_taxa_list = toy_missing_taxa_list_with_allowed_states,
   simmap = toy_simmap, allowed_states = c("blue", "green"),
   method = "BD_clades", verbose = FALSE, seed = 1234)

# Plot output phylogeny with colored grafts
plot_grafted(grafted_phy_states, use_simmap = FALSE, cex = 0.7)

# The updated simmap is in the output:
# the grafted taxa are in the state of the point where they were grafted.
# Grafted branches are dashed lines, and grafted tips are labelled in red.
plot_grafted(grafted_phy_states, fsize = 0.5, ftype = "i",
             simmap_colors = c("blue" = "dodgerblue", "green" = "limegreen", "red" = "firebrick"))

# States of the grafted taxa: "Genus_a_sp1" and "Genus_c_sp2" can be "red"
# thanks to their taxon-level states, but the taxa using the global states cannot.
all_states <- phytools::getStates(grafted_phy_states$simmap, type = "tips")
all_states[toy_missing_taxa_list_with_allowed_states$Taxon_name]


# --- Use several stochastic maps (alternative hypotheses of trait evolution) as constraints --- #

# A 'multiSimmap' gives several stochastic maps of the same trait:
# the grafting is run with each of them.
# Here, 3 maps are simulated from the states of the tips of the toy simmap.
tip_states <- phytools::getStates(toy_simmap, type = "tips")

set.seed(118)
toy_multiSimmap <- phytools::make.simmap(toy_backbone, tip_states,
                                         model = "ER", nsim = 3, message = FALSE)

# Graft using constraints from all 3 simmaps X 2 simulations per simmap
grafted_phys_maps <- graft_missing_taxa(phy = toy_backbone,
    missing_taxa_list = toy_missing_taxa_list_with_allowed_states,
    simmap = toy_multiSimmap, allowed_states = c("blue", "green"),
    method = "BD_clades", nb_simulations = 2, verbose = FALSE, seed = 1234)

# Inspect output:
# A list with one element per simmap, each a multiPhylo with the simulations of the map
names(grafted_phys_maps)
names(grafted_phys_maps$map_2)
# The map and the seed of a phylogeny
attr(grafted_phys_maps$map_2[[1]], "map")
attr(grafted_phys_maps$map_2[[1]], "seed")


# --- Constraints from several traits at once --- #

# Load the simmap for the second trait: 'toy_simmap_elevation' has two states: "high" and "low".
data(toy_simmap_elevation)
# Load missing taxa list with '$allowed_states' covering both color and elevation traits
data(toy_missing_taxa_list_with_two_traits)
# NULL means no restriction for this taxon, for the given trait.
toy_missing_taxa_list_with_two_traits$allowed_states

# Global 'allowed_states': "blue" or "green" for colors, no restriction on the elevation.
# Each taxon can only be grafted on branch pieces in allowed states for both traits.
grafted_phy_traits <- graft_missing_taxa(phy = toy_backbone,
    missing_taxa_list = toy_missing_taxa_list_with_two_traits,
    simmap = list(color = toy_simmap, elevation = toy_simmap_elevation),
    allowed_states = list(c("blue", "green"), NULL),
    verbose = FALSE, seed = 1234)

# '$simmap' is a list of updated simmaps, one per trait
names(grafted_phy_traits$simmap)


# --- Constraints from biogeographic ranges and another trait at once --- #

# One of the traits can be treated as biogeographic ranges:
# the allowed ranges do not have to match exactly the range of target stem branch pieces,
# any allowed range comprised in a target range is valid (e.g., "A" can be grafted on "AB").
# Here, the first trait holds ranges.

# Load datasets
data(toy_simmap_ranges) # Ranges simmap
data(toy_missing_taxa_list_with_ranges_and_elevation) # Taxon-level constraints
toy_missing_taxa_list_with_ranges_and_elevation$allowed_states

# Global 'allowed_states': ranges containing "A" or "B", no restriction on the elevation.
grafted_phy_ranges_traits <- graft_missing_taxa(phy = toy_backbone,
    missing_taxa_list = toy_missing_taxa_list_with_ranges_and_elevation,
    simmap = list(biogeo = toy_simmap_ranges, elevation = toy_simmap_elevation),
    allowed_states = list(c("A", "B"), NULL),
    treat_states_as_ranges = c(biogeo = TRUE, elevation = FALSE),
    verbose = FALSE, seed = 1234)

# '$simmap' is a list of updated simmaps, one per trait: ranges, and elevation
names(grafted_phy_ranges_traits$simmap)

# Check the constraints of both traits (columns 'states_ok_<trait>' and 'ranges_ok')
check_grafting_constraints(phy_grafted = grafted_phy_ranges_traits, phy_backbone = toy_backbone,
    missing_taxa_list = toy_missing_taxa_list_with_ranges_and_elevation,
    allowed_states = list(c("A", "B"), NULL),
    treat_states_as_ranges = c(biogeo = TRUE, elevation = FALSE))


}
